java is a statically typed language - a variable's type is fixed at compile time.

3.9 The 8 Primitive Data Types

Primitives store the actual value directly. Everything else (String, arrays, your classes) is a reference type.
 That's covered in later modules.

Type	Size	Range / Precision	Default use	Literal example
byte	8 bits	−128 to 127	raw binary data, file/network bytes	byte b = 10;
short	16 bits	−32,768 to 32,767	rarely used	short s = 1000;
int	32 bits	about −2.1 billion to 2.1 billion (−2³¹ to 2³¹−1)	default for whole numbers	int n = 42;
long	64 bits	about ±9.2 × 10¹⁸	IDs, timestamps in ms, large counts, money in paise	long t = 1_700_000_000_000L;
float	32 bits	about 6–7 significant digits	rarely in backend work	float f = 3.14f;
double	64 bits	about 15–16 significant digits	default for decimals (measurements, ratios)	double d = 3.14;
char	16 bits	0 to 65,535 (one UTF-16 code unit)	single character	char c = 'A';
boolean	JVM-dependent	true / false	flags, conditions	boolean active = true;

Critical details:

Integer literals are int by default. long x = 3000000000; fails to compile ("integer number too large")
because 3 billion doesn't fit in int. Write 3000000000L (use capital L; a lowercase l looks like 1).
Decimal literals are double by default. float f = 3.14; fails to compile. Write 3.14f.
Underscores improve readability: 1_000_000 equals 1000000.
char is a number internally. 'A' is 65, 'a' is 97, '0' is 48. So 'A' + 1 evaluates to the int 66.
boolean is not a number. Unlike C, boolean b = 0; doesn't compile, and you can't cast between int and boolean.
